        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Standby vs Line-Interactive vs Online Double-Conversion
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every uninterruptible power supply does the same basic thing: it keeps a battery charged and feeds your equipment from that battery when wall power fails. The three designs differ in what they do the rest of the time, and that difference decides both the price and which equipment they are suitable for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standby (Offline)</h3>

          <p>
            A standby UPS, sometimes called offline, simply passes wall power through to your devices until it detects a failure, then switches to its inverter. The switch takes a few milliseconds, which computer power supplies ride through easily. Standby units are cheap and efficient, but they do nothing about low or high voltage short of switching to battery, and most of them produce a stepped, simulated sine wave on battery. The APC BE600M1 is a standby unit, and that is fine for a router.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Line-Interactive: The Right Choice for Most Homes</h3>

          <p>
            A line-interactive UPS adds an autotransformer that boosts or trims the voltage during brownouts and swells without touching the battery. This is the design almost every home and small office should buy. It handles the common real-world problems, a sagging voltage on a hot afternoon, a flicker during a storm, a two-minute outage, while keeping the battery for actual outages. The better line-interactive units, including the CyberPower CP1500PFCLCD, APC BR1500MS2, APC SMT1500C and Eaton 5SC1500, also produce a pure sine wave on battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Online Double-Conversion</h3>

          <p>
            An online double-conversion UPS rectifies all incoming power to DC and inverts it back to AC continuously, so the output is always generated fresh by the inverter. There is no transfer time and no waveform change during an outage, and the output is isolated from voltage and frequency problems on the input. The trade-offs are heat, fan noise, lower efficiency and price. It is the right choice for servers, sensitive audio and video gear and anyone who runs their house from a portable generator, whose output can wander enough to confuse a line-interactive unit.
          </p>

