        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. VA vs Watts: How to Size a UPS Correctly
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">VA Is Not Watts</h3>

          <p>
            UPS model names are built around volt-amps, but the number that limits what you can plug in is watts. VA is apparent power, the voltage times the current. Watts are real power, the energy your devices actually consume. The ratio between the two is the power factor, and consumer UPS units are typically rated at a power factor of about 0.6 to 0.7, which is why a 1,500VA box is usually rated 865W to 1,000W. Online units like the CyberPower OL1500RTXL2U run closer to 0.9, which is why the same 1,500VA badge carries a 1,350W rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size From Real Wattage Plus Headroom</h3>

          <p>
            To size a UPS, add up the real wattage of everything you will plug into the battery-backed outlets, then choose a unit whose watt rating is at least 25 to 30 percent higher. That headroom covers startup surges, graphics card power spikes and battery aging. Do not size from the wattage printed on your computer's power supply. A 750W power supply is a ceiling, not a draw; a gaming PC with one typically pulls 300W to 550W under a heavy game and far less at the desktop. A plug-in watt meter for about $20 gives you the real number in a minute.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Real Draw</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-40W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Modem, router and ONT</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">60-150W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Office desktop plus monitor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">350-600W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Gaming PC under load</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30-80W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Four-bay NAS</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Worked Examples</h3>

          <p>
            Here is how that works in practice. An office desktop drawing 90W, a 30W monitor and a 25W router total about 145W, which fits a 600W unit like the CyberPower CP1000PFCLCD with enormous headroom and long runtime. A gaming PC peaking at 550W, two 40W monitors and the router total around 655W, which needs a 1,000W unit such as the CP1500PFCLCD. A home lab with a NAS, a small server and a switch drawing 300W continuously is the case for a Smart-UPS, an Eaton 5SC or an online unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Keep Off the Battery Outlets</h3>

          <p>
            Do not plug laser printers, space heaters or anything with a heating element into a battery-backed outlet. A laser printer's fuser can draw 800W to 1,000W for a few seconds when it warms up, enough to overload a consumer UPS and trip it offline at exactly the wrong moment. Put printers on the surge-only outlets or a separate strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, size for the load you will actually back up, not for everything on your desk. Speakers, desk lamps, phone chargers and the printer can live on surge-only outlets. Fewer watts on the battery side means more minutes of runtime, and runtime is the whole point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Runtime Tables: How Long Will a UPS Actually Last?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Runtime Is Shorter Than You Expect</h3>

          <p>
            UPS runtime surprises almost everyone, usually unpleasantly. A typical 1,500VA consumer unit carries two 12V sealed lead-acid batteries with a nominal capacity of roughly 200Wh. Lead-acid batteries deliver far less than their nominal capacity when discharged quickly, and the inverter loses another 10 to 15 percent, so at heavy loads you get only a fraction of that 200Wh. That is why manufacturers quote runtimes of a few minutes at full load and why the half-load figure is often three to four times longer, not twice as long.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Approximate Runtimes for a 1,500VA Unit</h3>

          <p>
            The table below shows approximate runtimes for a typical 1,500VA line-interactive unit with a fresh battery, based on the shape of manufacturer runtime charts for this class. Treat the numbers as planning figures. Your unit's published runtime chart is the authority, and runtime falls as the battery ages.
          </p>

