              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shutdown Tool vs Hold-Over Tool</h3>

          <p>
            The lesson is that a UPS is a shutdown tool for computers and a hold-over tool for network gear. Five minutes at 600W is plenty to save your work and shut down a gaming PC, and PowerPanel or PowerChute can do it automatically if you are away. Two hours at 30W is enough to keep the internet up through most short outages. Neither is a substitute for a generator or a large battery during a multi-hour outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want both, the efficient approach is two devices: a small UPS for the network stack, which draws little and benefits from long runtime, and a 1,000W or 1,500VA unit for the computer, which only needs enough time to shut down. The APC Smart-UPS SMT1500C is an exception worth noting because its larger internal battery is rated for about 23 minutes at a 500W half load, roughly double what most consumer units manage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Options for Longer Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            For longer runtimes, some business UPS models accept external battery packs, including the CyberPower OL1500RTXL2U. For most homes, a portable power station with a UPS mode is the cheaper way to buy hours rather than minutes, which we cover below.
          </p>
