        <div id="sine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pure Sine Wave and Active PFC Power Supplies
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Simulated vs Pure Sine Wave</h3>

          <p>
            If you are buying a UPS for a desktop computer, the output waveform on battery matters more than any other spec. Cheaper standby units produce a stepped approximation of a sine wave. Better line-interactive and all online units produce a pure sine wave that looks like utility power. We explain the electrical difference in detail in our guide to <a href="/pure-sine-wave-vs-modified-sine-wave/">pure sine wave vs modified sine wave</a>, but the short version for computers comes down to one component.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Active PFC Power Supplies Care</h3>

          <p>
            Nearly every desktop power supply sold in the past decade uses active power factor correction, or active PFC. Efficiency standards such as 80 Plus effectively require it. Active PFC circuits are designed for a smooth sine wave, and some of them react badly to a stepped waveform at the moment of transfer: the power supply can shut down, reboot or emit a loud buzz. The result is a UPS that beeps reassuringly while the computer it is supposed to protect turns off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every active PFC supply fails on a simulated sine wave, and plenty of owners run office PCs on cheap standby units without trouble. But you cannot know in advance, and the price difference between a simulated-sine 1,500VA unit and a pure sine wave model like the CyberPower CP1500PFCLCD is small enough that it is not worth gambling. If the computer cost more than about $500, buy pure sine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Network Gear Is the Exception</h3>

          <p>
            Network equipment is the exception. Modems, routers, ONTs and switches run from small external power adapters or simple internal supplies that do not care about waveform. That is why the APC BE600M1 is a perfectly good router UPS even though we would not put a gaming PC on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Check a Model</h3>

          <p>
            To check a model, look for the words pure sine wave or sinewave in the spec sheet. CyberPower's PFC Sinewave line, APC's Back-UPS Pro BR series, Smart-UPS and Eaton's 5SC series all qualify. Simulated sine, stepped approximation to a sine wave and modified sine all mean the cheaper waveform.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="router" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Keeping Your Internet Up: Router and Modem Backup
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Network Matters Most</h3>

          <p>
            For many households, the most valuable thing a UPS can protect is not the computer but the internet connection. Work calls, home security cameras and smart thermostats all depend on the modem and router, and a power blink that reboots them can take three to five minutes to recover from even when the outage itself lasted one second.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Router UPS Options</h3>

          <p>
            The simplest backup battery for a router is a small standby UPS like the APC BE600M1 plugged in where the modem, router and any fiber ONT live. A typical network stack draws 20W to 40W, so even a 600VA unit runs it for the better part of an hour, and a 1,500VA unit for two or more. Put the modem, router, ONT and any network switch on battery outlets; leave everything else off.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a category of mini DC UPS units designed specifically for routers. Instead of producing AC, they sit between the router's 12V power adapter and the router itself and feed it DC from a small lithium battery. APC's Back-UPS Connect is the best-known example. Because there is no inverter conversion loss, a small battery goes further, and the units are about the size of a paperback. The catch is that they are typically rated for one 12V device, so a separate modem, router and ONT may need two or three of them, and you need to match the connector and voltage of each adapter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Your Provider's Equipment Matters Too</h3>

          <p>
            One limitation applies regardless of the UPS: your internet only stays up if your provider's equipment does. Fiber networks are largely passive between your home and the central office, so fiber service often survives local outages. Cable networks rely on powered amplifiers and nodes in the neighborhood, many of which have limited battery backup. If your cable internet goes down every time the power does even with a UPS on the modem, the problem is upstream, and a cellular hotspot is your backup, not a bigger battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the network stack is your main concern and outages in your area last hours rather than minutes, a power station is the better tool. A 1,000Wh unit running a 30W network load lasts more than a full day, which no consumer UPS can match.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Power Station UPS Mode: The Long-Runtime Alternative
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How UPS Mode Works on a Power Station</h3>

          <p>
            Many current portable power stations advertise a UPS or EPS mode: plug the power station into the wall, plug your equipment into the power station, and it switches to battery when the grid fails. With 1,000Wh to 2,000Wh of LiFePO4 capacity, that turns minutes of runtime into hours. A 1,024Wh unit at about 85 percent usable delivers around 870Wh, which runs a 200W office setup for about four hours and a 30W network stack for more than a day. See our ranking of the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> for capacity and price comparisons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Transfer-Time Catch</h3>

          <p>
            The catch is transfer time. A true UPS either never transfers, in the online case, or transfers in a few milliseconds. Power station makers typically quote switchover times of 10 to 30 milliseconds. Computer power supplies are designed to hold up for roughly 16 milliseconds or more under load, so a 10ms switchover is usually fine and a 30ms switchover may or may not be, depending on the power supply and how heavily it is loaded. Owners commonly report that desktops survive switchover on some units and reboot on others.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Loads That Suit a Power Station</h3>

          <p>
            That makes power stations excellent for loads that tolerate a brief interruption or that have their own batteries: routers, modems, laptops, CPAP machines, refrigerators, aquariums and sump pumps. We cover the medical-device case in <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">can a portable power station run a CPAP</a>. For a desktop computer where any reboot means lost work, a real UPS is still the safer front line.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chaining a UPS and a Power Station</h3>

          <p>
            The best of both worlds is to chain them: plug a small line-interactive UPS into a power station. The UPS covers the instant of transfer with its own battery and conditions the output, and the power station supplies hours of energy behind it. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best portable power station for home backup</a> covers which models handle continuous pass-through charging well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power stations also use LiFePO4 batteries rated for thousands of cycles, compared with a few hundred deep cycles for the sealed lead-acid batteries inside most UPS units. The trade-off is that leaving any battery at 100 percent on the wall for years is not ideal; units that let you set a charge limit, around 80 to 90 percent, will age more gracefully in a permanent UPS role. Our <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion</a> comparison explains why chemistry matters for always-on duty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. UPS Battery Life, Replacement and Lithium Models
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Long a Lead-Acid Battery Lasts</h3>

          <p>
            The battery in a consumer UPS is a sealed lead-acid pack, and it is the part that wears out. Expect three to five years of service in a normal room, less in a hot closet. Heat is the main enemy: every sustained increase of about 15 degrees Fahrenheit above room temperature roughly halves lead-acid battery life. Do not install a UPS next to a radiator, in an unventilated cabinet or on the floor of a hot garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Ignore the Self-Test Warning</h3>

          <p>
            Most UPS units run a periodic self-test and warn you with a light, a beep or a software alert when the battery is weak. Do not ignore it. A failing battery often still shows as charged until the moment it is needed, then collapses under load in seconds. If your UPS is three years old and you have never tested it, unplug it from the wall while your computer is running and see what happens. It is far better to learn the battery is dead on a calm afternoon than during a storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Replacement Batteries</h3>

          <p>
            Every pick on our list has a user-replaceable battery. APC sells replacement battery cartridges, CyberPower and Eaton sell their own kits, and generic batteries of the same voltage, capacity and terminal type are widely available at lower prices. A replacement battery typically costs a fraction of a new UPS, so a unit with a good inverter and electronics is worth keeping for ten years or more. Recycle the old battery; auto parts stores and many electronics retailers take sealed lead-acid batteries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lithium UPS Models and Outlet Count</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion and LiFePO4 UPS units are beginning to appear, including models from APC and CyberPower aimed at network closets. They are lighter, last longer and tolerate heat better, but they cost substantially more for the same rating. For a home office, the economics still favor lead-acid with a battery swap every four years, unless the UPS lives somewhere hot or hard to reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, buy a UPS with a battery-backed outlet count that matches your needs today plus one. Running out of battery outlets and chaining a power strip off the UPS is a common way to overload it with devices that never needed backup in the first place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Surge Protection, Shutdown Software and Setup
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surge Protection Limits</h3>

          <p>
            Every UPS on our list includes surge suppression on all outlets, but a UPS is not a substitute for proper surge protection at the panel. A plug-in surge suppressor absorbs a limited amount of energy, and a large surge from a nearby lightning strike or utility fault can overwhelm it. A whole-house surge protective device installed at the breaker panel takes the first hit and reduces what reaches the UPS. Our guide to the <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">best whole-house surge protectors</a> covers that layer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Install the Shutdown Software</h3>

          <p>
            Install the shutdown software. CyberPower's PowerPanel, APC's PowerChute and Eaton's software all connect over the included USB data cable and can save your work and shut the computer down when the battery reaches a threshold you choose. Without it, an outage while you are away simply ends with the battery running flat and the computer losing power uncleanly, which is exactly what you bought the UPS to prevent. NAS devices from Synology, QNAP and others support USB UPS signaling natively, and multiple NAS boxes or servers can share one UPS through network UPS tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plugging In Correctly</h3>

          <p>
            Plug the UPS directly into a wall outlet, not into a power strip or surge protector. Daisy-chaining can confuse the UPS's line sensing, may void its warranty and adds another failure point. Plug the computer, monitor and network gear into battery outlets and the printer, speakers and lamps into the surge-only outlets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Generators and Alarms</h3>

          <p>
            If you run your house from a portable generator during outages, test the UPS on generator power before you need it. Many open-frame generators produce voltage and frequency that drift enough to make a line-interactive UPS switch to battery repeatedly, draining it while the generator is running. Inverter generators produce cleaner power and usually work fine. If you depend on a conventional generator, an online double-conversion UPS solves the problem completely. Our guide to the <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">best inverter generators</a> covers clean-power options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Last, turn off the alarm if it bothers you, but leave the alerts on in software. The beeping is there to tell you the UPS is on battery and the clock is running; a silent UPS with no software alerts gives you no warning at all before it shuts down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best backup battery supply for a computer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most desktop computers, a 1,500VA line-interactive UPS with a pure sine wave output, such as the CyberPower CP1500PFCLCD. It is rated 1,000W, handles active PFC power supplies cleanly, corrects brownouts without draining the battery and gives you several minutes to save and shut down. Office PCs drawing under 300W can use the smaller CP1000PFCLCD.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between VA and watts on a UPS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                VA is apparent power, voltage times current; watts are the real power your equipment consumes. Consumer UPS units have a power factor of roughly 0.6 to 0.7, so a 1,500VA model is usually rated 865W to 1,000W. Always size by watts, and keep your actual load at least 25 percent below the watt rating to leave room for spikes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a UPS run my computer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on load. A typical 1,500VA unit runs a 200W office setup for roughly 20 to 30 minutes and a 600W gaming PC for only a few minutes. A 30W modem and router can run two hours or more. Check the runtime chart for your exact model and expect the numbers to fall as the battery ages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a pure sine wave UPS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a desktop computer with an active PFC power supply, which covers almost every modern PC, yes. Stepped or simulated sine wave output can make those supplies shut down at the moment of transfer. Routers, modems and laptops on their chargers are fine on simulated sine wave, which is why cheap standby units remain a good choice for network gear.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best backup battery for a router?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small standby UPS like the APC BE600M1 is the simplest choice and runs a typical 20W to 40W modem and router for a long stretch. Mini 12V DC UPS units are more efficient for a single router but need matching connectors. If your outages last hours, a portable power station in UPS mode runs network gear for a day or more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station work as a UPS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, many have a UPS or EPS mode, and they offer hours of runtime instead of minutes. The trade-off is switchover time, typically quoted at 10 to 30 milliseconds, which some desktop power supplies tolerate and some do not. They are ideal for routers, CPAP machines and refrigerators. For a desktop PC, put a small UPS between the power station and the computer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace a UPS battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every three to five years for the sealed lead-acid batteries in most consumer and small-business units, sooner if the UPS lives somewhere hot. Replace it when the unit's self-test or software warns you, or when runtime noticeably drops. Replacement batteries cost a fraction of a new UPS, so there is rarely a reason to throw out a working unit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by VA instead of watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500VA badge does not mean 1,500W. Most consumer units are rated 865W to 1,000W, and that watt figure is the real limit. Add up the actual draw of what you will plug into battery outlets, measure it with a watt meter if you can, and leave 25 to 30 percent headroom for spikes and battery aging.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a gaming PC on a simulated sine wave unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Active PFC power supplies, which are in almost every modern desktop, can shut off or reboot when a standby UPS switches to its stepped waveform. The UPS beeps, the computer turns off, and you lose your work anyway. For any desktop worth protecting, pay the small premium for a pure sine wave model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging a laser printer into battery outlets</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A laser printer's fuser can draw close to 1,000W for several seconds when it warms up. On a consumer UPS that is enough to trigger an overload alarm or shut the UPS down entirely. Laser printers, space heaters and anything with a heating element belong on the surge-only outlets or a separate strip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never testing or replacing the battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealed lead-acid UPS batteries last three to five years, and a weak one can look fine until it collapses under load. Run the self-test, heed the replace-battery warning, and once a year pull the plug from the wall with the computer running. A dead UPS battery is worse than no UPS at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting hours of runtime</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500VA UPS running a gaming PC lasts minutes, not hours, because lead-acid batteries deliver little capacity at high draw. Use a UPS to ride through flickers and shut down cleanly. If you need to keep working through a long outage, pair it with a power station or a generator instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

